Below is a **complete, practical play‑book** you can follow (or adapt) to build a modern, production‑ready frontend for an e‑commerce site.  
I’ll walk you through the whole journey – from planning to deployment – and give you concrete code snippets, tooling choices, and best‑practice tips that work for most projects today.

---

## 1️⃣ Planning & Architecture

| Step | What to do | Why it matters |
|------|------------|----------------|
| **Define core features** | Product catalog, search, filters, product detail page, cart, checkout, user account, reviews, admin panel, analytics, recommendation engine. | Gives you a clear scope. |
| **Choose a tech stack** | <ul><li>Framework: React + Next.js (SSR + SSG + API routes)</li><li>State: Redux Toolkit + RTK Query (or Zustand + React Query)</li><li>Styling: Tailwind CSS + Headless UI (or Chakra UI)</li><li>Type safety: TypeScript</li><li>Testing: Jest + React Testing Library + Cypress</li></ul> | Balances developer experience, performance, and ecosystem maturity. |
| **Design system** | Create a Figma/Sketch design system with tokens (colors, spacing, typography). Export tokens to JSON → Style Dictionary → CSS variables. | Keeps UI consistent and scalable. |
| **API contract** | Decide between REST or GraphQL. For e‑commerce, GraphQL (Apollo Client) gives you fine‑grained queries; REST (Axios) is simpler if you already have a REST backend. | Determines how you fetch data. |
| **Performance strategy** | • Server‑Side Rendering (SSR) for SEO.<br>• Static Generation (SSG) for product pages.<br>• Code‑splitting & lazy‑loading for heavy components.<br>• Image optimization (Next/Image). | Improves load time, SEO, and UX. |
| **Accessibility & SEO** | • Semantic HTML, ARIA roles.<br>• Meta tags, structured data (JSON‑LD).<br>• Lighthouse audit. | Makes your site usable and discoverable. |

---

## 2️⃣ Project Setup

```bash
# 1️⃣ Create a new Next.js app with TypeScript
npx create-next-app@latest ecommerce-frontend --typescript

cd ecommerce-frontend

# 2️⃣ Add Tailwind CSS
npm install -D tailwindcss postcss autoprefixer
npx tailwindcss init -p

# 3️⃣ Configure Tailwind (tailwind.config.js)
module.exports = {
  content: [
    "./pages/**/*.{js,ts,jsx,tsx}",
    "./components/**/*.{js,ts,jsx,tsx}"
  ],
  theme: { extend: {} },
  plugins: []
}

# 4️⃣ Add global styles (styles/globals.css)
@tailwind base;
@tailwind components;
@tailwind utilities;

# 5️⃣ Install other dev dependencies
npm i -D eslint prettier eslint-config-prettier eslint-plugin-prettier
npm i @reduxjs/toolkit react-redux @tanstack/react-query axios
npm i @heroicons/react
npm i jest @testing-library/react @testing-library/jest-dom @testing-library/user-event
npm i cypress
```

> **Tip:** Use `pnpm` or `yarn` if you prefer a lockfile that’s faster to install.

---

## 3️⃣ Folder Structure

```
/components   # Reusable UI components
/pages        # Next.js pages (routes)
/store        # Redux Toolkit slices & store
/services     # API clients (axios or Apollo)
/hooks        # Custom hooks (e.g., useCart, useAuth)
/utils        # Utility functions
/styles       # Global CSS, Tailwind config
/tests        # Jest unit tests
/cypress      # Cypress end‑to‑end tests
```

---

## 4️⃣ Core Components & Features

### 4.1 Product Card (React + Tailwind)

```tsx
// components/ProductCard.tsx
import Link from 'next/link';
import Image from 'next/image';

export interface ProductCardProps {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  price: number;
